#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
ood-usage-collect: aggregate Open OnDemand Apache access logs into a
long-lived SQLite database, so usage history outlives log rotation.

Run as root (the Apache logs are root-readable) from cron or a systemd
timer. It is safe to run as often as you like: every log file is
fingerprinted by its first line and read from a stored byte offset, so a
line is never counted twice, across rename rotation, copytruncate, and
later compression (.gz/.bz2/.xz) alike.

Only daily aggregates are stored (day, user, category, item, count).
Raw log lines are never copied into the database.

When Slurm accounting is available it also records wall time, core time,
and GPU time of finished interactive app jobs, found by job name. By default
it uses Slurm only if an OnDemand cluster config uses the Slurm adapter.

Requires Python 3.6+ and SQLite 3.24+ (EL8/EL9 stock versions are fine).
"""

